    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "content": "## 2. Engineering Standards: ISO 13851 Classification & The Physics of Distance\n\nPoint-of-operation safeguarding by two-hand control is governed by **ISO 13851** (*Safety of machinery — Two-hand control devices*). The standard defines five distinct types based on the severity of the hazard:\n\n| ISO 13851 Type | Required Concurrence | Anti-Tie-Down (Release of both) | Synchronous Actuation (≤500 ms) | Cross-Monitoring & Cat 4 / PL e |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Type I** | Yes | No | No | No (Single channel) |\n| **Type II** | Yes | Yes | No | No |\n| **Type III A** | Yes | Yes | Yes (≤ 500 ms) | Cat 1 or Cat 3 |\n| **Type III B** | Yes | Yes | Yes (≤ 500 ms) | Cat 3 / PL d |\n| **Type III C** | **Yes (Continuous)** | **Yes (Mandatory)** | **Yes (Mandatory)** | **Yes (Category 4 / PL e)** |\n\nFor metalforming presses, shears, and guillotine cutters, **Type III C** is the absolute legal requirement.\n\n### The Safety Distance Equation (EN ISO 13855)\n\nA two-hand control device does not physically block an operator from reaching into the die bed; it protects the operator by keeping both hands occupied on the console while hazardous motion is occurring. Therefore, the console must be mounted far enough from the hazard that the operator cannot release a button and reach the pinch point before the press comes to a complete standstill:\n\n```\nS = (K × T) + C\n```\n\nWhere:\n- **S:** Minimum safety distance in millimeters.\n- **K:** Human hand approach speed constant ($1,600\\text{ mm/s}$ per ISO 13855).\n- **T:** Total stopping time of the machine in seconds ($t_{\\text{valve}} + t_{\\text{hydraulic}} + t_{\\text{controller}}$).\n- **C:** Depth penetration factor ($250\\text{ mm}$ minimum for two-hand consoles without physical shrouds; $0\\text{ mm}$ if buttons have surrounding physical ring guards).\n\nIf a press takes 250 milliseconds ($0.25\\text{ s}$) to stop its ram after a button is released:\n`S = (1600 × 0.25) + 0 = 400\\text{ mm}`. Mounting the console any closer constitutes a direct regulatory violation."
    },
